window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "index.html", "title": "Java 9 → 26 — everything new, version by version", "headings": ["Release timeline", "Jump in", "⚡ Quick start", "📦 Browse by version", "🔬 Deep-dive topics", "Deep-dive topics", "var", "Records", "Sealed classes", "Pattern matching & switch", "Text blocks & Strings", "Modules (JPMS)", "Virtual threads", "Structured concurrency", "Scoped values", "Streams & Gatherers", "Foreign Function & Memory"], "text": "A hands-on reference Java 9 → 26 — everything new, version by version A quick way to learn what changed in Java from 9 to 26 — organised by language features , new APIs , tooling and JVM/other changes, with tiny before → after snippets so you can see the old way and the new way side by side. How the versions work. Java ships every 6 months, but only some releases are LTS (long-term support). This guide has a page for each major stepping-stone release — 9, 11, 17, 21, 25, 26 — and folds the features of the in-between releases into the next stepping stone, always tagged with the version that introduced them (e.g. Added in 14 ). So Java 17 also covers the good stuff from 12–16, Java 21 covers 18–20, and Java 25 covers 22–24. Release timeline Sept 2017 Java 9 Modules (JPMS), JShell, collection factory methods, reactive Flow, HTTP/2 client (incubator). Sept 2018 · LTS Java 11 covers 10 var (from 10), standard HttpClient, run single-file source, new String/Files methods. Sept 2021 · LTS Java 17 covers 12–16 Records, sealed classes, switch expressions, text blocks, pattern matching for instanceof , helpful NPEs. Sept 2023 · LTS Java 21 covers 18–20 Virtual threads, pattern matching for sw var instanceof"}, {"url": "quickstart.html", "title": "Quick start & tooling", "headings": ["1 · Install a JDK", "2 · Install & switch between versions", "SDKMAN! (macOS / Linux / WSL) — recommended", "jenv (per-directory switching)", "Homebrew (macOS)", "Manual — set JAVA_HOME", "3 · JShell — the Java REPL Added in 9", "4 · Run a single source file — no compile step Added in 11", "Multi-file source programs Added in 22", "Shebang scripts", "5 · Try preview language features", "6 · Try incubator modules", "7 · Handy everyday commands", "30-second cheat sheet"], "text": "Set up your machine Quick start & tooling Install one or more JDKs, switch between them, and learn the fastest ways to try features — JShell, single-file programs, preview features and incubator modules. 1 · Install a JDK Pick a build of OpenJDK. Any of these give you the same language; they differ in support/branding: Eclipse Temurin (Adoptium) — the popular community default. Oracle JDK — Oracle's build. Amazon Corretto , Azul Zulu , Microsoft / Liberica — vendor builds, often with extras. Verify what you have: java -version # runtime version javac -version # compiler version java --list-modules | head # JDK modules available 2 · Install & switch between versions You will want several JDKs installed at once (e.g. 17, 21, 25). Use a version manager. SDKMAN! (macOS / Linux / WSL) — recommended curl -s \"https://get.sdkman.io\" | bash # install SDKMAN sdk list java # see available builds sdk install java 25-tem # install Temurin 25 sdk install java 21.0.5-tem sdk use java 25-tem # switch for THIS shell only sdk default java 21.0.5-tem # set the global default jenv (per-directory switching) brew install jenv jenv add /Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines/temurin-25.jdk/Contents/Home jenv g java -version # runtime version javac -version # compiler version java --list-modules | head # JDK modules available curl -s \"https://get.sdkman.io\" | bash # install SDKMAN sdk list java # see available builds sdk install java 25-tem # install Temurin 25 sdk install java 21.0.5-tem sdk use java 25-tem # switch for THIS shell only sdk default java 21.0.5-tem # set the global default brew install jenv jenv add /Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines/temurin-25.jdk/Contents/Home jenv global 21 # default jenv local 25 # pin THIS project (writes .java-version) brew install openjdk@25 openjdk@21 openjdk@17 export JAVA_HOME=$(/usr/libexec/java_home -v 25) export PATH=\"$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH\" # Linux: JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/temurin-25 $env:JAVA_HOME = \"C:\\Program Files\\Java\\jdk-25\" $env:Path = \"$env:JAVA"}, {"url": "features.html", "title": "Feature index", "headings": [], "text": "A–Z reference Feature index Every notable change from Java 9 to 26 in one filterable table — what it is, when it landed, whether it's final, and where to read more. Type in the box to filter. Feature Introduced Status Category Learn more Modules (JPMS) 9 Final Language / platform Modules deep-dive Private interface methods 9 Final Language Java 9 Try-with-resources on effectively-final vars 9 Final Language Java 9 var — local-variable type inference 10 Final Language var deep-dive var in lambda parameters 11 Final Language var deep-dive Switch expressions 14 Final Language Pattern matching Text blocks 15 Final Language Text blocks Records 16 Final Language Records deep-dive Pattern matching for instanceof 16 Final Language Pattern matching Sealed classes & interfaces 17 Final Language Sealed classes Pattern matching for switch 21 Final Language Pattern matching Record patterns (destructuring) 21 Final Language Pattern matching Unnamed variables & patterns _ 22 Final Language Pattern matching Flexible constructor bodies 25 Final Language Java 25 Module import declarations 25 Final Language Java 25 Compact source files & instance main 25 Final Language Java 25 Primitive types in patt var var instanceof switch _ main List.of takeWhile dropWhile ofNullable Flow Optional ifPresentOrElse or stream isBlank strip repeat lines Files.readString writeString Collectors.teeing Stream.toList() mapMulti HexFormat jlink java File.java jpackage jwebserver @snippet synchronized"}, {"url": "versions/java-9.html", "title": "Java 9", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Private methods in interfaces", "Try-with-resources on effectively-final variables", "Enhanced @Deprecated", "Small clean-ups: diamond on anonymous classes · @SafeVarargs on private methods · _ banned", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Immutable collection factory methods", "Stream additions: takeWhile , dropWhile , ofNullable , ranged iterate", "New Collectors: filtering & flatMapping", "Optional : ifPresentOrElse , or , stream", "CompletableFuture : timeouts & delays", "Stack-Walking API", "Reactive Streams — java.util.concurrent.Flow", "Process API — inspect & control OS processes", "3 · Tooling", "JShell — the REPL", "jlink · multi-release JARs · unified logging", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 9 in one glance"], "text": "September 2017 Java 9 The big structural release: the module system (JPMS) reorganised the JDK itself. Alongside it came JShell , collection factory methods , a reactive Flow API, CompletableFuture timeouts, the Stack-Walking API and many smaller language tidy-ups. Modules JPMS — modular JDK (~90 modules) JShell official REPL List.of() immutable collection factories Flow reactive streams jlink custom runtime images ⚠ Migration watch-outs (8 → 9). Strong encapsulation of JDK internals — reflective access to sun.* /internal APIs now warns (and later fails). Run jdeps --jdk-internals your.jar first. Split packages are illegal on the module path — two JARs sharing a package break. _ is no longer a valid identifier. New version string ( 9 , not 1.9 ) — regexes that parsed 1.x break. Applet API and several GC flag combinations deprecated. 1 · Language features Private methods in interfaces Why it matters: Java 8 gave interfaces default methods, but there was no way to share logic between them without exposing it. private (and private static ) interface methods fix that. Before (Java 8) interface Logger { default void info(String m) { log(\"INFO\", m); } default void error(String m) { log(\" CompletableFuture sun.* jdeps --jdk-internals your.jar _ 9 1.9 1.x default private private static interface Logger { default void info(String m) { log(\"INFO\", m); } default void error(String m) { log(\"ERROR\", m); } // shared helper is forced to be public default void log(String lvl, String m) { System.out.println(lvl + \": \" + m); } } interface Logger { default void info(String m) { log(\"INFO\", m); } default void error(String m) { log(\"ERROR\", m); } private void log(String lvl, String m) { // hidden helper System.out.println(lvl + \": \" + m); } } // must declare a fresh variable inside the () BufferedReader reader = openReader(); try (BufferedReader r = reader) { return r.readLine(); } BufferedReader reader = openReader(); // effectively final try (reader) { // use it directly return reader"}, {"url": "versions/java-11.html", "title": "Java 11 LTS", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "var — local-variable type inference Added in 10", "var in lambda parameters Added in 11", "Nest-based access control Added in 11", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Standard HTTP Client — java.net.http", "New String methods", "Files: read/write a whole string in one call", "Immutable copies & unmodifiable collectors Added in 10", "Other handy additions", "3 · Tooling", "Run a single source file — no compile step JEP 330", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 11 (LTS) in one glance"], "text": "September 2018 · LTS Java 11 LTS The first LTS after 8 and the baseline most teams migrated to. Headliners: var (introduced in 10), the standard HttpClient , running a single source file without compiling, and a batch of convenient String / Files / Collection methods. var type inference (from 10) HttpClient standard, HTTP/2 + async String isBlank / strip / repeat / lines java App.java run source, no compile LTS covers Java 10 Folded in: features from Java 10 are included here and tagged, since 10 was a non-LTS stepping-stone. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (8 → 11). Java EE & CORBA modules removed — java.xml.bind (JAXB), java.xml.ws (JAX-WS), java.activation , java.corba are gone. Add them as explicit Maven/Gradle dependencies. The #1 upgrade break from 8. JavaFX is no longer bundled — use OpenJFX as a separate dependency. Nashorn JavaScript engine deprecated (removed in 15). No more JRE / Server JRE downloads — ship a runtime with jlink . 1 · Language features var — local-variable type inference Added in 10 Why it matters: drops the redundant left-hand type when it's obvious from the initializer. It's still static typing — no runtime cost. Before Map<String, List<Integer>> scores = new Ha var String Files Collection java.xml.bind java.xml.ws java.activation java.corba jlink var Map<String, List<Integer>> scores = new HashMap<String, List<Integer>>(); for (Map.Entry<String, List<Integer>> e : scores.entrySet()) { } var scores = new HashMap<String, List<Integer>>(); for (var e : scores.entrySet()) { } // type inferred var var map.forEach((k, v) -> use(k, v)); // can't annotate k or v map.forEach((@NonNull var k, @NonNull var v) -> use(k, v)); private javac java.net.http HttpURLConnection URL url = new URL(\"https://api.example.com\"); HttpURLConnection c = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection(); c.setRequestMethod(\"GET\"); try (var in = new BufferedReader( new InputStreamReader(c.getInputStream()))) { String body = in.lines().collect(joining(\"\\n\")); } HttpClient client = Http"}, {"url": "versions/java-17.html", "title": "Java 17 LTS", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Switch expressions Final in 14", "Records Final in 16", "Sealed classes Final in 17", "Pattern matching for instanceof Final in 16", "Text blocks Final in 15", "Pattern matching for switch Preview in 17", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Stream.toList() Added in 16", "Stream.mapMulti — one-to-many Added in 16", "Compact number formatting Added in 12", "Unix-domain socket channels Added in 16", "Other API additions", "3 · Tooling", "Helpful NullPointerExceptions Added in 14", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 17 (LTS) in one glance"], "text": "September 2021 · LTS Java 17 LTS A landmark LTS. Across 12–17 the language gained modern data modelling — records , sealed classes , switch expressions , text blocks and pattern matching — turning verbose boilerplate into a few readable lines. records data carriers (16) sealed closed hierarchies (17) switch expressions (14) + patterns \"\"\" text blocks (15) LTS covers 12–16 Folded in: features from 12 13 14 15 16 are included and tagged with the version that introduced them. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (11 → 17). JDK internals strongly encapsulated by default (JEP 403) — the old --illegal-access=permit escape hatch is gone. Reflective access to internals now fails; add explicit --add-opens / --add-exports only where needed. Older bytecode libraries (ASM, cglib, Mockito) may need upgrading. Security Manager deprecated for removal ; RMI Activation removed ; experimental AOT/JIT (Graal) compiler removed. Always-strict floating point restored — math may differ slightly from pre-17 non- strictfp results. 1 · Language features Switch expressions Final in 14 Why it matters: switch can return a value, uses arrow labels (no fall-through), and yield for blocks — eliminating a whole class of break b --illegal-access=permit --add-opens --add-exports strictfp switch yield break int days; switch (month) { case JAN: case MAR: case MAY: days = 31; break; case FEB: days = 28; break; default: days = 30; } int days = switch (month) { case JAN, MAR, MAY -> 31; case FEB -> 28; default -> 30; }; // exhaustive, no break, single assignment equals hashCode toString public final class Point { private final int x, y; public Point(int x, int y){this.x=x;this.y=y;} public int x(){return x;} public int y(){return y;} public boolean equals(Object o){ /* ... */ } public int hashCode(){ /* ... */ } public String toString(){ /* ... */ } } public record Point(int x, int y) { } switch public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Rectangle { } public record Circle(double r) implements Shape { } publi"}, {"url": "versions/java-21.html", "title": "Java 21 LTS", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Virtual threads Final in 21", "Pattern matching for switch Final in 21", "Record patterns Final in 21", "Previews that landed here", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Sequenced collections Added in 21", "Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM) API Added in 21", "3 · Tooling", "Code snippets in Javadoc — @snippet Added in 18", "Simple Web Server & Generational ZGC", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 21 (LTS) in one glance"], "text": "September 2023 · LTS Java 21 LTS The concurrency-defining LTS. Virtual threads make blocking code scale to millions of tasks, while pattern matching for switch and record patterns complete Java's data-oriented programming story. Plus sequenced collections and generational ZGC. virtual threads millions of cheap threads switch patterns + guards, final record patterns destructuring Sequenced getFirst/getLast/reversed LTS covers 18–20 Folded in: features from 18 19 20 are included and tagged. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (17 → 21). UTF-8 is the default charset since 18 — code relying on the platform default (file reads/writes, new String(bytes) ) can change behaviour. Set charsets explicitly. Virtual-thread pinning: on 21–23, blocking inside synchronized pins the carrier and can starve the pool. Prefer ReentrantLock around blocking I/O (fixed in 24). Dynamic loading of agents warns ; finalization deprecated for removal 18 — migrate to Cleaner . String templates tried in 21 previews were later withdrawn — don't depend on them. 1 · Language features Virtual threads Final in 21 Why it matters: write simple blocking code that scales to millions of concurrent tasks — the runtime frees the OS thre new String(bytes) synchronized ReentrantLock Cleaner var pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(200); for (var task : tasks) pool.submit(task); // ~200 concurrent; more = queueing or OOM try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) { for (var task : tasks) ex.submit(task); } // millions of concurrent blocking tasks, cheap switch String s; if (obj instanceof Integer i) s = \"int \" + i; else if (obj instanceof String str) s = \"str \" + str; else s = \"other\"; String s = switch (obj) { case Integer i when i > 100 -> \"big int \" + i; case Integer i -> \"int \" + i; case String str -> \"str \" + str; default -> \"other\"; }; if (obj instanceof Point p) { int x = p.x(); int y = p.y(); } if (obj instanceof Point(int x, int y)) { } switch (shape) { case Line(Point(var x1,var y1), Point(var x2,va"}, {"url": "versions/java-25.html", "title": "Java 25 LTS", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Compact source files & instance main Final in 25", "Flexible constructor bodies Final in 25", "Module import declarations Final in 25", "Unnamed variables & patterns Final in 22", "Scoped values Final in 25", "Still previewing in 25", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Stream Gatherers Final in 24", "Foreign Function & Memory API Final in 22", "Cryptography", "3 · Tooling", "Markdown in Javadoc Added in 23", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 25 (LTS) in one glance"], "text": "September 2025 · LTS Java 25 LTS The current LTS. A wave of features graduated to final : scoped values , stream gatherers , the Foreign Function & Memory API , compact source files & instance main , flexible constructor bodies and module import declarations — plus real startup wins from Project Leyden. void main() compact source + IO scoped values final (ThreadLocal successor) gatherers custom stream ops (24) FFM native calls, no JNI (22) LTS covers 22–24 Folded in: features from 22 23 24 are included and tagged. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (21 → 25). Security Manager permanently disabled 24 — System.setSecurityManager now throws. Rework any code still using it. sun.misc.Unsafe memory-access methods warn and are being removed — migrate to the FFM API / VarHandle . 32-bit x86 port removed ; compact object headers change object sizes (tools may need updating). Upside: virtual threads no longer pin on synchronized 24 — drop the 21-era ReentrantLock workarounds. 1 · Language features Compact source files & instance main Final in 25 Why it matters: a runnable program with no class , no public static , no explicit java.base imports, and a simple IO helper — much gentler for learners and scri System.setSecurityManager sun.misc.Unsafe VarHandle synchronized ReentrantLock main class public static java.base IO public class Hello { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(\"Hello, world\"); } } void main() { IO.println(\"Hello, world\"); } // java Hello.java — no class, no static, IO built in super(...) this(...) Range(int lo, int hi) { super(); // must be first statement if (lo > hi) // too late — super already ran throw new IllegalArgumentException(); this.lo = lo; this.hi = hi; } Range(int lo, int hi) { if (lo > hi) // guard BEFORE super() throw new IllegalArgumentException(); super(); this.lo = lo; this.hi = hi; } import java.util.List; import java.util.Map; import java.util.stream.Collectors; import java.util.function.Function; import module java.base; // all j"}, {"url": "versions/java-26.html", "title": "Java 26", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Prepare to Make final Mean Final JEP 500", "Primitive types in patterns, instanceof & switch Preview (4th)", "Structured concurrency Preview (6th)", "2 · New & updated APIs", "HTTP/3 for the HTTP Client JEP 517", "Lazy Constants Preview (2nd)", "Also previewing / incubating", "3 · Tooling", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 26 in one glance"], "text": "March 2026 Java 26 A feature release that pushes performance and platform integrity forward: HTTP/3 in the HTTP client, more AOT startup caching , a faster G1 , and the \" final means final \" integrity work — while structured concurrency and primitive patterns edge closer to final. Non-LTS. Java 26 is a 6-month feature release. Production teams on LTS should track these as previews of what's coming ; the next LTS will consolidate them. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (25 → 26). \"final means final\" (JEP 500) — libraries that mutate final fields via setAccessible now warn and will later fail. Watch older serialization, mocking and dependency-injection frameworks. Applet API removed Removed — any lingering applet code will not compile/run. Preparing to restrict JNI — native access continues to tighten; expect to pass --enable-native-access . 1 · Language features Prepare to Make final Mean Final JEP 500 Part of the \"integrity by default\" effort. Historically, libraries could mutate final fields via deep reflection ( setAccessible ). Java 26 begins warning on this so a future release can enforce that final truly is final — enabling constant-folding optimisations and safer code. Before — silently final setAccessible --enable-native-access final final setAccessible final Field f = obj.getClass().getDeclaredField(\"id\"); f.setAccessible(true); f.setInt(obj, 999); // mutates a final field — worked // same code now emits a warning; a future release // will throw. Opt back in per-module only if needed: // --enable-final-field-mutation=<module> instanceof switch Object o = 42; switch (o) { case int i -> System.out.println(\"int \" + i); case double d -> System.out.println(\"double \" + d); default -> {} } // and safe range checks: if (bigValue instanceof int i) { ... } // only matches if it fits in int java.net.http.HttpClient HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder() .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2) .build(); HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder() .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_"}, {"url": "topics/var.html", "title": "var — local-variable type inference Java 10", "headings": ["1 · It's still static typing", "2 · Where you can use it", "3 · Where you cannot use it", "4 · Rules & gotchas", "5 · var vs Object", "6 · Style: when to use it", "7 · Real-world examples", "8 · Cheat sheet", "var in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · language · complete guide var — local-variable type inference Java 10 var lets the compiler infer a local variable's type from its initializer. It is syntactic sugar, not dynamic typing — the variable still has one fixed, static type; you simply don't write it out. static still fully typed (not dynamic) 0 runtime cost — pure compile-time locals only (+ lambda params in 11) less boilerplate for long generics Java 10 (lambda params: 11) The problem it solves Long generic type names had to be written twice — once on the left, once on the right — ( Map<String, List<Customer>> m = new HashMap<…>() ), adding noise without adding information. var removes the redundant left-hand type when it's obvious from the initializer, without any change to runtime behaviour or performance. On this page 1. It's still static typing 2. Where it works 3. Where it doesn't 4. Rules & gotchas 5. var vs Object 6. Style: when to use it 7. Real-world examples 8. Cheat sheet 1 · It's still static typing var s = \"hi\"; compiler infers type = String bytecode: String no \"var\", no cost s = 42; ✗ won't compile — s is a String forever var is resolved entirely at compile time. The variable's type is fixed to var var Map<String, List<Customer>> m = new HashMap<…>() var var Map<String, List<Customer>> byCity = new HashMap<String, List<Customer>>(); for (Map.Entry<String, List<Customer>> e : byCity.entrySet()) { } try (BufferedReader r = Files.newBufferedReader(path)) { } var byCity = new HashMap<String, List<Customer>>(); for (var e : byCity.entrySet()) { } try (var r = Files.newBufferedReader(path)) { } for (var i = 0; i < 10; i++) { } list.forEach((var s) -> print(s)); // lambda param (Java 11+) var for var x; // ✗ no initializer — nothing to infer var y = null; // ✗ null has no type var f = () -> 42; // ✗ lambda needs a target type var arr = { 1, 2, 3 }; // ✗ array initializer needs a type public var field = 1; // ✗ fields cannot use var var method() { } // ✗ return types cannot use var void"}, {"url": "topics/records.html", "title": "Records Final in Java 16", "headings": ["1 · What is a record?", "2 · What the compiler generates", "3 · Customising records", "Compact canonical constructor — validate / normalise", "Extra members that are allowed", "4 · Immutability & defensive copies", "5 · Records + pattern matching", "6 · Records vs class / enum / Lombok", "7 · Serialization", "8 · Pitfalls", "9 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — DTO + multiple return values", "10 · Cheat sheet", "Records in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · language · complete guide Records Final in Java 16 A record is a concise, immutable, transparent carrier for a fixed set of values. You declare the components ; the compiler generates the constructor, accessors, equals , hashCode and toString — correctly, every time. 1 line replaces ~40 of boilerplate 5 members auto-generated 100% immutable by default ✓ destructurable with patterns Java 16 final (preview in 14) The problem it solves \"Data classes\" (DTOs, value objects, tuples) were almost all boilerplate — and hand-written equals / hashCode are easy to get subtly wrong (forget a field and you get silent bugs). Records make that boilerplate automatic and correct , and clearly signal intent: this type is just its data . On this page 1. What is a record? 2. What the compiler generates 3. Customising records 4. Immutability & defensive copies 5. Records + pattern matching 6. Records vs class / enum / Lombok 7. Serialization 8. Pitfalls 9. Real-world use cases 10. Cheat sheet 1 · What is a record? You write the shape of the data in the header — the record components — and that's it. The record is implicitly final , its fields are final , and it carries no hidden state beyond record equals hashCode toString equals hashCode final final public final class Point { private final int x, y; public Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; } public int x() { return x; } public int y() { return y; } @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { if (!(o instanceof Point p)) return false; return x == p.x && y == p.y; } @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(x, y); } @Override public String toString() { return \"Point[x=\" + x + \", y=\" + y + \"]\"; } } public record Point(int x, int y) { } enum get equals hashCode toString x() y() equals hashCode toString Point[x=1, y=2] private final java.lang.Record final public Range(int lo, int hi) { if (lo > hi) throw new IllegalArgumentException(); this.lo = lo; this.hi = hi; } public record Range(int lo, int hi) { pub"}, {"url": "topics/sealed-classes.html", "title": "Sealed classes & interfaces Final in Java 17", "headings": ["1 · What & why", "2 · The three choices each subtype must make", "3 · Rules", "4 · The payoff: exhaustive switch", "5 · Sealed + records = algebraic data types", "6 · Sealed vs enum vs abstract class", "7 · Pitfalls", "8 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — a typed Result instead of exceptions", "9 · Cheat sheet", "Sealed classes in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · language · complete guide Sealed classes & interfaces Final in Java 17 A sealed type declares exactly which classes may extend or implement it. That gives you a closed, known set of subtypes — the foundation for exhaustive pattern matching and for modelling \"a value is one of a fixed set of shapes\". closed known subtype set 0 default needed in switch compile-time exhaustiveness check 3 subtype modes: final / sealed / non-sealed Java 17 final (preview in 15) The problem it solves Before, a type was either final (no subtypes) or completely open (anyone, anywhere could subclass). There was no way to say \"these three types and no others\", so the compiler could never know a hierarchy was complete — every switch needed a default and could silently miss a newly added case at runtime. Sealed types close the hierarchy so the compiler can reason about it. On this page 1. What & why 2. final / sealed / non-sealed 3. Rules 4. The payoff: exhaustive switch 5. Sealed + records = ADTs 6. Sealed vs enum vs abstract 7. Pitfalls 8. Real-world use cases 9. Cheat sheet 1 · What & why Legacy — open hierarchy, compiler can't help public abstract class Shape { } // Any code anywhere can do: c sealed default final switch default public abstract class Shape { } // Any code anywhere can do: class Blob extends Shape {} // so every switch over Shape must have a default and // can silently miss new subtypes → runtime surprises. public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Rectangle { } public record Circle(double r) implements Shape { } public record Square(double side) implements Shape { } public record Rectangle(double w, double h) implements Shape { } final Quadrilateral sealed Rectangle non-sealed final sealed permits non-sealed permits sealed interface Expr { } // permits inferred below record Num(int v) implements Expr { } record Add(Expr l, Expr r) implements Expr { } final permits switch default double area(Shape s) { if (s instanceof Circle c) return Math.PI*c.r()*"}, {"url": "topics/pattern-matching-switch.html", "title": "Pattern matching & switch", "headings": ["1 · The family & timeline", "2 · Switch expressions Java 14", "3 · Pattern matching for instanceof Java 16", "4 · Pattern matching for switch + guards Java 21", "5 · Record patterns (destructuring) Java 21", "6 · Unnamed patterns & variables _ Java 22", "7 · Primitive type patterns Preview", "8 · Dominance & exhaustiveness", "9 · Pitfalls", "10 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — render a JSON value", "11 · Cheat sheet", "Pattern matching in one glance"], "text": "Deep dive · language · complete guide Pattern matching & switch A family of related features that let you test a type, bind a variable and destructure data in one step — turning long if / instanceof / cast chains and fall-through switch statements into concise, exhaustive, readable expressions. 3-in-1 test + cast + bind 0 default over sealed/enum nested record destructuring 14→25 built up over releases Java 21 switch patterns final The problem it solves Working with a value whose concrete type varies used to mean a ladder of if (x instanceof T) + cast + extract, or a switch riddled with break and fall-through bugs. Pattern matching makes \"what is this, and pull it apart\" a first-class, compiler-checked operation — the basis of data-oriented programming in Java. On this page 1. The family & timeline 2. Switch expressions 3. instanceof patterns 4. Switch patterns + guards 5. Record patterns 6. Unnamed patterns _ 7. Primitive patterns 8. Dominance & exhaustiveness 9. Pitfalls 10. Real-world use cases 11. Cheat sheet 1 · The family & timeline 14 switch expr 16 instanceof 21 switch patterns + record patterns 22–25 unnamed _ preview primitives Pattern matching arrived in pieces. Each bui if / instanceof / cast switch default if (x instanceof T) switch break switch yield instanceof switch case _ case int i int num; switch (day) { case MON: case TUE: case WED: case THU: case FRI: num = 5; break; // forget break → bug case SAT: case SUN: num = 2; break; default: throw new IllegalStateException(); } int num = switch (day) { case MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI -> 5; case SAT, SUN -> 2; }; // arrow: no fall-through; exhaustive over the enum // block form uses yield: int n = switch (day) { case MON -> { log(day); yield 5; } default -> 0; }; instanceof if (o instanceof String) { String s = (String) o; // repeat the type + cast if (s.length() > 3) use(s); } if (o instanceof String s && s.length() > 3) { use(s); // s bound where the test is true } if (!(o instanceof String s)) return; // "}, {"url": "topics/text-blocks-strings.html", "title": "Text blocks & modern String APIs Final in Java 15", "headings": ["1 · Text blocks basics", "2 · Indentation — incidental vs essential whitespace", "3 · Two special escapes for text blocks", "4 · Templating & interpolation", "5 · Modern String & text methods", "6 · Pitfalls", "7 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — a SQL query & a JSON body", "8 · Cheat sheet", "Text blocks & strings in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · language + API · complete guide Text blocks & modern String APIs Final in Java 15 Text blocks are multi-line string literals delimited by \"\"\" — no more \\n , escaped quotes or + concatenation for JSON, SQL, HTML and friends. Alongside them, a set of String methods added across 9–15 fill long-standing gaps. 0 escaped quotes / \\n 0 + concatenation Unicode strip vs ASCII trim 10+ new String methods (9–15) Java 15 text blocks final The problem it solves Embedding SQL, JSON, HTML or regex in Java meant a wall of \"...\" + \"\\n\" + and \\\" escapes — hard to read, easy to break, painful to copy-paste. Text blocks let you paste the literal content as-is. The new String methods remove the endless little utility helpers everyone re-wrote ( isBlank , repeat , lines …). On this page 1. Text blocks basics 2. Indentation (incidental whitespace) 3. Special escapes \\ and \\s 4. Templating & interpolation 5. Modern String methods 6. Pitfalls 7. Real-world use cases 8. Cheat sheet 1 · Text blocks basics Legacy — escapes & concatenation String html = \"<html>\\n\" + \" <body>\\n\" + \" <p>Hello, \\\"world\\\"</p>\\n\" + \" </body>\\n\" + \"</html>\\n\"; Text block String html = \"\"\" <html> <body> <p>Hello, \"world\"< \"\"\" \\n + String \\n + strip trim \"...\" + \"\\n\" + \\\" String isBlank repeat lines String html = \"<html>\\n\" + \" <body>\\n\" + \" <p>Hello, \\\"world\\\"</p>\\n\" + \" </body>\\n\" + \"</html>\\n\"; String html = \"\"\" <html> <body> <p>Hello, \"world\"</p> </body> </html> \"\"\"; \"\"\" \" \\t \\n \\uXXXX \"\"\" \"\"\" String s = \"\"\" one two \"\"\"; // → \"one\\n two\\n\" (8-space margin removed; the extra 2 before \"two\" kept) // \\ at end of line — suppress the newline (join lines) String oneLine = \"\"\" Lorem ipsum dolor \\ sit amet\"\"\"; // \"Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet\" // \\s — a space that is NOT stripped as trailing whitespace String padded = \"\"\" key =\\s \"\"\"; // keeps the trailing space after '=' STR.\"...\" formatted String.format String msg = \"{\\n\" + \" \\\"user\\\": \\\"\" + name + \"\\\",\\n\" + \" \\\"age\\\": \" + age + \"\\n\" + \"}\"; String msg = \"\"\" { \""}, {"url": "topics/modules-jpms.html", "title": "The Java Platform Module System (JPMS) Java 9", "headings": ["1 · What & why", "2 · A module in one file: module-info.java", "3 · Directives at a glance", "4 · A module graph", "5 · exports vs opens — the key distinction", "6 · Classpath vs module path", "7 · Services (ServiceLoader)", "8 · Migration & escape hatches", "9 · Tooling: jlink & jdeps", "10 · Pitfalls", "11 · Real-world use cases", "12 · Cheat sheet", "Modules in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · platform · complete guide The Java Platform Module System (JPMS) Java 9 A module is a named, self-describing group of packages that declares what it needs ( requires ) and what it exposes ( exports ). It adds strong encapsulation and reliable configuration on top of the old flat classpath. ~90 modules the JDK is split into strong encapsulation (hidden = truly hidden) startup missing deps fail early, not deep in a run jlink ship only the modules you use Java 9 introduced (Project Jigsaw) The problem it solves The classpath is a flat bag of JARs: every public class is reachable by everyone (\"nothing is really private\"), missing or duplicate JARs blow up deep into a run (\"JAR hell\"), and you always ship the whole JRE. Modules make dependencies explicit and checked at startup, let libraries hide internals, and let tools assemble a minimal runtime. On this page 1. What & why 2. module-info.java 3. Directives 4. A module graph 5. exports vs opens 6. Classpath vs module path 7. Services (ServiceLoader) 8. Migration & escape hatches 9. Tooling: jlink, jdeps 10. Pitfalls 11. Real-world use cases 12. Cheat sheet 1 · What & why Strong encapsulation — packages you don't export are  requires exports export jlink module-info.java // src/com.myapp/module-info.java module com.myapp { requires java.net.http; // I depend on this module requires transitive com.mylib; // and my consumers get com.mylib too exports com.myapp.api; // this package is public API exports com.myapp.internal to com.myapp.tests; // qualified export uses com.myapp.spi.Plugin; // I consume this service provides com.myapp.spi.Plugin with com.myapp.plugins.DefaultPlugin; // I provide an implementation opens com.myapp.model; // allow deep reflection (frameworks) } requires M requires transitive M requires static M exports P exports P to M1, M2 opens P opens P to M uses S provides S with Impl ServiceLoader requires exports opens exports com.myapp.api; // Compile against & call public types normally. // De"}, {"url": "topics/virtual-threads.html", "title": "Virtual threads Final in Java 21", "headings": ["1 · What is a virtual thread?", "2 · Platform vs virtual threads", "3 · Architecture & how it works", "4 · Creating virtual threads", "5 · Executors & task fan-out", "6 · Lifecycle & mount/unmount", "7 · Pinning (and the JDK 24 fix)", "8 · Relationship with structured concurrency", "9 · Relationship with scoped values", "10 · Virtual threads in Spring Boot", "11 · Virtual threads vs WebFlux / reactive", "12 · Pitfalls & anti-patterns", "13 · Monitoring & debugging", "14 · Migration guide", "15 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — check 10,000 URLs concurrently", "16 · Cheat sheet", "Virtual threads in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · concurrency · complete guide Virtual threads Final in Java 21 Virtual threads are extremely cheap threads managed by the JVM instead of the operating system. They let you keep writing simple, blocking, one-thread-per-task code while scaling to millions of concurrent tasks — the performance of async, with the simplicity of plain threads. 1M+ concurrent virtual threads < 1 KB memory per thread (vs ~1 MB) < 1 µs to create (sub-microsecond) 0 OS threads wasted while blocked 100% plain blocking code (no rewrite) The problem it solves Servers used to face a lose-lose choice: thread-per-request code was simple but capped at a few thousand OS threads, while reactive/async code scaled but was hard to write and debug. Virtual threads end the trade-off — you write ordinary blocking, thread-per-request code and it still scales to hundreds of thousands of concurrent requests, because blocking a virtual thread no longer wastes an OS thread. On this page 1. What is a virtual thread? 2. Platform vs virtual threads 3. Architecture & how it works 4. Creating virtual threads 5. Executors & task fan-out 6. Lifecycle & mount/unmount 7. Pinning (and the JDK 24 fix) 8. With structured concurr // You size a pool and guard it, because OS // threads are scarce and blocking wastes them. ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(200); for (Request r : requests) { pool.submit(() -> { var user = userService.load(r.userId()); // blocks! var orders = orderService.list(r.userId()); // blocks! return render(user, orders); }); } // > 200 in-flight requests → they queue and latency spikes. // One virtual thread per request. Blocking is fine: // the carrier is freed while this VT waits. try (var exec = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) { for (Request r : requests) { exec.submit(() -> { var user = userService.load(r.userId()); // blocks cheaply var orders = orderService.list(r.userId()); // blocks cheaply return render(user, orders); }); } } // 100k+ concurrent requests,"}, {"url": "topics/structured-concurrency.html", "title": "Structured concurrency Preview", "headings": ["1 · The problem with raw futures", "2 · The scope model", "3 · Basic usage", "4 · Joiners — choosing the completion policy", "5 · Deadlines & cancellation", "6 · With virtual threads & scoped values", "7 · Pitfalls", "8 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — aggregate a product page with a 2s SLA", "9 · Cheat sheet", "Structured concurrency in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · concurrency · complete guide Structured concurrency Preview Structured concurrency treats a group of related subtasks as a single unit of work . They start together inside a scope, and the scope guarantees they all finish — or are all cancelled — before the block exits. No leaked threads, clear error propagation, honest task trees. no leaks all subtasks joined/cancelled fail-fast one error cancels siblings 1 VT per subtask (cheap) clear parent-child task tree Preview 6th preview in Java 26 The problem it solves Firing off subtasks with a raw ExecutorService + Future s is unstructured : if one task fails, siblings keep running (wasted work / leaks), cancellation is manual and easy to botch, and there's no relationship between parent and child in stack traces or thread dumps. Structured concurrency gives those subtasks a lifetime bound to a lexical scope — like try-with-resources, but for concurrency. Still a preview feature (5th preview in Java 25, 6th in 26). Compile/run with --enable-preview . The API here is the reworked Java 25 shape; earlier releases used ShutdownOnFailure / ShutdownOnSuccess (see Evolution). On this page 1. The problem with raw futures 2. The scope ExecutorService Future --enable-preview ShutdownOnFailure ShutdownOnSuccess Future<User> u = pool.submit(() -> findUser(id)); Future<Order> o = pool.submit(() -> fetchOrder(id)); try { return new Response(u.get(), o.get()); // if findUser throws, fetchOrder keeps running (leak); // if the caller is interrupted, both may be orphaned. } catch (Exception e) { u.cancel(true); o.cancel(true); // did you remember both? throw e; } try (var scope = StructuredTaskScope.open()) { // Java 25 API var user = scope.fork(() -> findUser(id)); var order = scope.fork(() -> fetchOrder(id)); scope.join(); // wait for all; first failure cancels the rest return new Response(user.get(), order.get()); } // on exit, every subtask is guaranteed done Response handle(String id) throws Exception { try (var scope = St"}, {"url": "topics/scoped-values.html", "title": "Scoped values Final in Java 25", "headings": ["1 · Why not ThreadLocal?", "2 · Declare, bind, read", "3 · How binding works", "4 · run / call / multiple / rebinding", "5 · With structured concurrency", "6 · Pitfalls", "7 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — propagate user + trace id through a request", "8 · Cheat sheet", "Scoped values in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · concurrency · complete guide Scoped values Final in Java 25 A ScopedValue shares immutable data with code further down the call stack without passing it as a parameter — a modern, safer, virtual-thread-friendly replacement for many uses of ThreadLocal . immutable no set() to misuse auto unbound at scope end inherited by forked subtasks 0 leaks no remove() to forget Java 25 final (preview in 21) The problem it solves ThreadLocal is mutable (anyone can set() it any time), leaks unless you remember remove() , and is copied into every child thread — painful with millions of virtual threads . Scoped values give you the same \"ambient context\" without those hazards: bound for a well-defined scope, immutable, and cleaned up automatically. On this page 1. Why not ThreadLocal? 2. Declare, bind, read 3. How binding works 4. run / call / multiple / rebinding 5. With structured concurrency 6. Pitfalls 7. Real-world use cases 8. Cheat sheet 1 · Why not ThreadLocal? ThreadLocal ScopedValue Mutability Mutable any time ( set ) Immutable; bound for a scope only Lifetime Until remove() (leaks if forgotten) Auto-unbound when the block exits Inheritance Copied into child threads (costly) Sh ScopedValue ThreadLocal set() remove() ThreadLocal set() remove() set remove() static final ThreadLocal<User> CURRENT = new ThreadLocal<>(); void handle(Request r) { CURRENT.set(authenticate(r)); try { process(); // deep code calls CURRENT.get() } finally { CURRENT.remove(); // MUST remember or you leak / see stale data } } static final ScopedValue<User> CURRENT = ScopedValue.newInstance(); void handle(Request r) { ScopedValue.where(CURRENT, authenticate(r)) .run(() -> process()); // bound only inside run(...) } // automatically unbound here — nothing to clean up // declare once (shared, final) static final ScopedValue<User> CURRENT = ScopedValue.newInstance(); // bind for a scope ScopedValue.where(CURRENT, user).run(() -> process()); // read anywhere inside that scope void process() { Us"}, {"url": "topics/streams-gatherers.html", "title": "Stream Gatherers Final in Java 24", "headings": ["1 · Built-in gatherers", "2 · Before & after: windowing", "3 · Anatomy of a gatherer", "4 · Writing your own — \"distinct by key\"", "5 · mapConcurrent — bounded parallel I/O in a stream", "6 · Other recent Stream additions worth knowing", "7 · Pitfalls", "8 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — batch records for a bulk insert", "9 · Cheat sheet", "Gatherers in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · API · complete guide Stream Gatherers Final in Java 24 A Gatherer is a custom intermediate stream operation — the long-missing counterpart to a custom Collector . Where collect ends a stream, gather transforms it mid-pipeline: windowing, running totals, dedup-by-key, bounded-concurrency mapping and more. custom intermediate operations 5 built-in gatherers lazy & composable in a pipeline bounded concurrency via mapConcurrent Java 24 final (preview in 22) The problem it solves Streams shipped with a fixed set of intermediate ops ( map , filter , limit …). Anything stateful across elements — sliding windows, running averages, \"stop after N distinct\", batching — meant leaving the stream for manual loops. Collector let you customise the end ; Gatherer finally lets you customise the middle . On this page 1. Built-in gatherers 2. Before & after: windowing 3. Anatomy of a gatherer 4. Writing your own 5. mapConcurrent 6. Other recent Stream additions 7. Pitfalls 8. Real-world use cases 9. Cheat sheet 1 · Built-in gatherers import java.util.stream.Gatherers; Stream.of(1,2,3,4,5).gather(Gatherers.windowFixed(2)).toList(); // [[1,2],[3,4],[5]] Stream.of(1,2,3,4).gather(Gatherers.wi Gatherer Collector collect gather map filter limit Collector Gatherer import java.util.stream.Gatherers; Stream.of(1,2,3,4,5).gather(Gatherers.windowFixed(2)).toList(); // [[1,2],[3,4],[5]] Stream.of(1,2,3,4).gather(Gatherers.windowSliding(2)).toList(); // [[1,2],[2,3],[3,4]] Stream.of(1,2,3,4).gather(Gatherers.scan(() -> 0, Integer::sum)).toList(); // [1,3,6,10] (running fold, emits each step) Stream.of(\"a\",\"b\",\"c\").gather(Gatherers.fold(() -> \"\", String::concat)).toList(); // [\"abc\"] (fold to one value, emitted at the end) urls.stream().gather(Gatherers.mapConcurrent(10, this::fetch)).toList(); // bounded-parallel map, order preserved (see §5) List<List<Integer>> windows = new ArrayList<>(); for (int i = 0; i + 3 <= xs.size(); i++) { windows.add(List.copyOf(xs.subList(i, i + 3))); } var"}, {"url": "topics/ffm-api.html", "title": "Foreign Function & Memory API Final in Java 22", "headings": ["1 · Why it replaces JNI", "2 · The two halves & call flow", "3 · Off-heap memory: Arena & MemorySegment", "4 · Calling native functions", "5 · Upcalls — pass a Java method as a C callback", "6 · jextract — generate bindings automatically", "7 · Native-access restrictions", "8 · Pitfalls", "9 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — read a large file off-heap without copying to the heap", "10 · Cheat sheet", "FFM in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · API · complete guide Foreign Function & Memory API Final in Java 22 The FFM API lets pure Java call native (C) functions and manage off-heap memory safely, with no hand-written C glue. It's the modern, supported replacement for JNI and the sun.misc.Unsafe memory tricks. 0 lines of JNI / C glue safe bounded, typed off-heap memory deterministic memory free via Arena jextract auto-generate bindings from headers Java 22 final (incubating since 14/17) The problem it solves Talking to native code meant JNI — hand-written C, compiled per platform, easy to crash the JVM — and off-heap memory meant the unsafe, leak-prone sun.misc.Unsafe . FFM replaces both with a typed, bounds-checked, pure-Java API where memory has an explicit, deterministic lifetime. On this page 1. Why it replaces JNI 2. The two halves & call flow 3. Off-heap memory (Arena) 4. Calling native functions 5. Upcalls (callbacks) 6. jextract — auto bindings 7. Native-access restrictions 8. Pitfalls 9. Real-world use cases 10. Cheat sheet 1 · Why it replaces JNI JNI (old) FFM (Java 22+) Native glue Hand-written C, compiled per platform None — pure Java Safety Easy to crash the JVM Bounds- & lifetime-checked Memory U sun.misc.Unsafe Arena sun.misc.Unsafe Unsafe Arena jextract Arena MemorySegment ByteBuffer buf = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(16) .order(ByteOrder.nativeOrder()); buf.putInt(0, 42); int v = buf.getInt(0); // no explicit lifetime; freed whenever the GC/cleaner gets to it try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) { MemorySegment seg = arena.allocate(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 4); seg.setAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 0, 42); int v = seg.getAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 0); } // memory released deterministically here ofConfined() ofShared() ofAuto() global() // call C's size_t strlen(const char *s); Linker linker = Linker.nativeLinker(); SymbolLookup std = linker.defaultLookup(); MethodHandle strlen = linker.downcallHandle( std.find(\"strlen\").orElseThrow(), FunctionDescriptor.of(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, /"}];
